{
	title: 'Tab strips: equal cells, underline, pages; full-width web',
	spec: `Tab strips in the terminal and on the web are one row of equal cells in real tab order, so Ctrl-N/P always move one cell right/left and no number ever shifts.

Cells: every cell has the same width: the widest tab number, right-aligned, plus one marker slot (task fr's glyph, or a blank of the same width) and a gap. The current tab never uses brackets or padding that would move other cells: in the terminal its number is underlined (colour of the active tab); on the web its cell is tinted with a lit bar under it (task gn). The terminal loses its [n] brackets.

Names: only when every tab fits with a name at one fixed width (number, marker, name clipped to that width) do cells show names; otherwise all cells are numbers. No per-tab close button in the strip (the web closes with Ctrl-W, /close or the full list).

Pages: when not even every number fits, tabs come in fixed pages of as many cells as fit beside two edge buttons: tabs 1..k, k+1..2k, and so on; the last page ends at the last tab and holds k tabs like the others (overlapping the one before), so no strip ends in empty room. The shown page is the one holding the current tab, a pure function of (tab count, current index, cells that fit), so going somewhere and back looks identical; moving inside a page moves only the underline, crossing a border replaces the row. The edge buttons have a fixed width (arrow plus marker slot) and show no count (no number on the outer perimeter): the left one reads ‹, the right one ›, followed by the most urgent marker among the tabs on that side (failed over asking or attention over working; finished marks do not count); an empty side's arrow is dimmed. Edges sit at the strip's ends, so the tabs run from almost the left border to almost the right one. Clicking an edge goes to the neighbouring page (the web; in the terminal they are display only). Ctrl-N on the last tab wraps to the first.

The paging arithmetic (which page, which edge counts, which most-urgent mark) lives in one common module used by both clients, tested as a pure function.

Web: the strip is one row, a menu button (the existing sheet: the full list with close buttons, now on every width) then the cells, edge buttons and new tab button; it never wraps and never scrolls sideways (the back-swipe rule of task ng). The whole header stays under 50px at any width. The current tab's name is in the status row already, so there is no title line.

Terminal: the tab bar has no 'Tabs:' label; it keeps its hints while they fit (task 7w), then drops them, then pages, and never clips a cell. Spare columns go before the right edge, which ends flush with the row.

Web transcript and composer use the full window width (minus padding), text included: no reading-width cap (the user: text often loves a wide screen). On touch devices a single tap activates a tab or page-edge link, never merely paints or focuses a hover state requiring another tap. Hover feedback is restricted to hover-capable fine pointers, and tab controls use native touch manipulation; links remain anchors so keyboard activation and desktop modified/middle clicks still work. When fixed-width tab cells leave spare room between the menu and new-tab controls, center their group (including page edges when present), rather than leaving all spare room after the last tab. Cell widths, ordering and paging stay unchanged.

The web tab-list sheet also offers a Reload page button: it reloads the current browser page explicitly (useful in an installed web app without browser chrome). Drafts and pending prompts survive the reload through the existing local/host draft stores; no timer or polling is involved. Opening the web tab-list dialog focuses its list container, not the first tab link: automatic focus must not paint an unrelated tab as selected. The non-interactive initial focus target has no outline and is absent from sequential keyboard navigation; pressing Tab moves to a real control whose visible focus outline remains. The current tab keeps its independent selected styling and scrolls into view; native Escape dismissal and focus return remain intact. Browser coverage checks initial focus and subsequent keyboard focus.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Mocks from the design discussion: /tmp/tabs-pages.html (the chosen paged strip) and /tmp/tabs-lens.html (rejected: variable-width cells and a sliding window made numbers move). Rejected also: centring on the current tab (every step shifts all numbers) and a lazily sliding window (the view depends on history).',
		"Implemented: src/common/tab-pages.ts (tabPages.page: names, numbers or a page, and the edges' counts and most urgent mark; widths in any unit, so the terminal passes columns and the web CSS px), used by src/client/tab-bar.ts and src/web/components/Tabs.tsx. The web measures its cell room with a ResizeObserver on .pages and a hidden 10-zero probe for 1ch; on pointer: coarse cells and edges are at least 44px, which the arithmetic simply gets as a bigger cell.",
		"Terminal cells show numbers only (7w's numeric labels); names are a web thing. The focused number is underlined with raw SGR 4/24, outside ansi.sgr, so it shows in a monochrome terminal too. Below about 16 columns a page (one cell plus two edges) no longer fits and the row overflows; no real terminal is that narrow.",
		"Edges are hidden with visibility on an empty side (the page size stays fixed) and display: none when not paged; a CSS display rule on .edge beats the hidden attribute, so .edge[hidden] needs its own display: none. Paging broke src/startup.test.ts's tab-bar regex (50 tabs at 120 columns now show the focused page 28..50), and the ng browser test now closes through the sheet. The sheet scrolls the shown tab into view when it opens.",
		"Terminal brought in line with the web (user, 2026-10-03): no 'Tabs:' label, edges are ‹ › plus mark with no count, both drawn when paged (empty side dimmed, like the web's disabled button), and the last page is full instead of trailing empty room. The web already had count-less edges; its strip scrolls natively rather than paging.",
		'Invariant: every web control acts on the first tap on iOS. Hover styles exist only under (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine), for all controls, not just tabs; the composer Send/Steer/Queue and + buttons took two taps on iPhone while they had touch hover and touch-action: none.'
	],
	needs: ['ng', '7w', 'fr']
}
